  "full_text": "Hello and welcome back to JD science. Today we are exploring how substances move in and out of cells. In diffusion particles move from high concentration to low concentration along a concentration gradient. It is faster when the temperature is higher, the concentration gradient is steeper or the surface area is larger. Oxygen diffuses into the blood in the lungs and carbon dioxide diffuses out. Alveoli have a large surface area, a thin lining, a good blood supply and ventilation. Osmosis is the diffusion of water across a partially permeable membrane. Active transport moves substances against a concentration gradient using energy from respiration. Root hair cells use active transport to absorb mineral ions. Diffusion and osmosis are passive processes. They do not require the cell to supply metabolic energy. Active transport is different because carrier proteins move substances against their concentration gradient using energy, often supplied by ATP. A useful comparison is oxygen entering cells by diffusion, water crossing a partially permeable membrane by osmosis and mineral ions entering root hair cells by active transport. As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process in your own words. Focus on what happens, why it happens and how the structures involved help the process work. Now pause the video and try this exam style question. Explain why a root hair cell uses active transport to absorb mineral ions? Here is a model answer. Mineral ions are often at a lower concentration in the soil than in the root cell, so active transport is needed to move them against the concentration gradient using energy from respiration. If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to JD Science for more lessons. You can find further learning resources and practice questions at jdscience.co.uk. Thank you for watching.",
